<div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;"><font color="#0f172a">	</font></span></div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div><br></div><div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div>&nbsp;Conscious change on 03/14 night</div><div>主要病史</div><div>&nbsp;<span style="white-space:pre">	</span></div><div>&nbsp;The patient was brought to the emergency department after a sudden collapse at home. According to family members, the patient had been in usual health until shortly before the event when he/she suddenly complained of a severe headache described as the “worst headache of life.” This was followed by abrupt loss of consciousness within minutes. No preceding trauma was reported.</div><div>Emergency medical services were activated immediately. During transport, the patient remained unresponsive. No seizure activity was clearly witnessed, though the family reported brief generalized body stiffening at the onset of collapse. There was no known history of recent fever, infection, or head injury.</div><div>Upon arrival to the emergency department, the patient was comatose with a Glasgow Coma Scale of E1M4V1 (total score 6). Initial vital signs showed BP 115/67 mmHg, pulse 100 bpm, respiratory rate 18 breaths/min, temperature 35.7°C, and oxygen saturation 99% on supplemental oxygen. Neurological examination demonstrated bilaterally small pupils measuring 1.0 mm with sluggish light reflexes and withdrawal to painful stimuli.</div><div>An emergent non-contrast head CT demonstrated diffuse subarachnoid hemorrhage involving the basal cisterns and sylvian fissures, highly suspicious for ruptured intracranial aneurysm. The CECT was performed and showed the Acom aneurysm rupture. Due to above reason, emergency surgery of EVD and ICP monitor was inserted. After surgery, he was sent to SICU for further management.\\<span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div><br></div><div><div><span style="white-space: pre; white-space: normal;">	</span></div><div>&nbsp;This is a 50-year-old female with aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) due to ruptured anterior communicating artery (AComA) aneurysm complicated by hydrocephalus. Initial severity was HH grade 5, modified Fisher grade 4, and WFNS grade 5. After admission, blood pressure was controlled within the range of 120-180 mmHg. Medications including levetiracetam, glycerol, vitamin K1, nimodipine, and tranexamic acid were administered. The patient's consciousness and neurological function gradually improved during this period. On 03/19, fever was noted. Fever survey was performed, and ceftriaxone was escalated to 1 vial every 12 hours (Q12H). The patient was closely monitored thereafter.</div><div><br></div><div>Persistent fever was noted during SICU care. Blood cultures showed no bacterial growth, and central fever was suspected. Follow-up brain CT on 03/26 demonstrated mild bilateral fronto-parietal SAH and intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH) in the lateral ventricles. The patient remained under close monitoring in the ICU with continued supportive management.</div><div><br></div><div>During ICU stay, intermittent fever persisted despite ceftriaxone treatment. Urine culture on 03/31 revealed Candida species, and fluconazole was added. However, on 04/02, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) culture and EVD tip culture both grew Staphylococcus species, suggestive of device-related infection. Infectious Disease (ID) was consulted. Fluconazole was discontinued, vancomycin was initiated, and ceftriaxone was continued. Under stable condition, the patient was transferred to the general ward on 04/02.</div><div>Neurological status: E4V3M6 (eye opening component noted)</div><div>Muscle power: RUL/LUL/RLL/LLL = 1/1/2/2 (unable to fully follow commands)</div><div><br></div><div>At the general ward, the patient's consciousness was E4V6M1. Vancomycin trough levels were regularly monitored, and dosing adjustments were made according to Infectious Disease recommendations. On 04/09, intermittent fever recurred. Fever workup was repeated, and results were pending. Further laboratory monitoring was arranged, including repeat vancomycin trough level on 04/11 and follow-up blood tests on 04/13.</div><div><br></div><div>On 04/16, vancomycin trough level was 11.29. Fever had subsided. Vancomycin was continued with adjusted dosing (1.5 g and 1 g alternating every 6 hours), as recommended by ID Dr.Huang, with a planned total duration of 14 days after EVD removal. Urinalysis on 04/16 showed 4+ findings; however, ID suggested possible contamination. Ceftriaxone was discontinued due to possible hepatic concerns, while vancomycin was continued. The patient's family expressed preference for rehab ward in Taipei, but not too far from here. On 04/17, dermatology was consulted for a newly developed widespread symmetric blanchable erythematous maculopapular rash over the trunk and proximal lower extremities. The eruption was partially confluent. (黃副覺得不像red man syndrome)</div><div>Dermatology consultation recommendations included:</div><div>- Topical mometasone BID for facial lesions until resolution or color change</div><div>- Topical steroid (Topsym) BID for trunk and limbs</div><div>- Oral diphenhydramine 1 tab every 8 hours</div><div>- Close monitoring for warning signs of severe cutaneous adverse drug reaction (SCAR)</div><div>The current goal is completion of antibiotic therapy and monitoring of infection and skin reaction status.</div><div><br></div><div>This week, we followed up lab tests regularly, showing better results. No fever was noted for one week, and also for the widespread erythematous maculopapular rash concern, we discontinued Vancomycin use and prescribed Hydrocortisone 100mg 1vial every 8 hours. During rehabilitation, vocal cord palsy was noted, so we consulted ENT. They suggested keep voice rehabilitation and follow up laryngoscopy 3 months later for bilateral vocal paresis, probably previous SAH related. Follow-up lab tests on 04/23 showed much better results. We gradually tapered Hydrocortisone dose on 04/24. The current goal remains monitoring of infection and skin reaction status.</div><div><br></div><div>This week, we continued current treatment. However, fever up to 38C was noted in the morning of 04/27. Fever survey was done, and antipyretics were given. In the meantime, her widespread erythematous maculopapular rash had mostly faded, but instead, newly formation of vesicles on the trunk noted was noted on 04/27. We consulted dermatology again for the scattered superficial vesicles, miliaria crystallina was suspected, and they suggested:&nbsp;</div><div>- Topical menphencala Lotion BID for trunk&nbsp;</div><div>- Prevent from over-heat or over-humidity.</div><div>Since then, the scattered superficial vesicles were much more relieved after topical lotion use. Also, fever was subsided after the vesicles diminished. Follow-up lab data on 04/30 was fair. Under relatively stable condition, she is being arranged for transfer to the Xizhi NS ward on 2026/05/02.</div></div></div><div></div>